# Generated artifacts that are committed on purpose but should not be read as review surface.
#
# `openapi.json` and `crates/api-client/src/lib.rs` are ~1 MB of `utoipa` → `progenitor` output
# in a repository whose hand-written source is a fraction of that (BUILD_AND_OPS §9.3), and
# `xtask openapi --check` is what guarantees they match the code — so a reviewer reading their
# diff is reading the same information twice, at length.
#
# `-diff` suppresses the diff body, not the fact that the file changed: `git diff --stat` and
# GitHub's file list still show it, and `git diff --text` still prints it when someone genuinely
# wants to look. `linguist-generated` keeps them out of language statistics and collapses them
# in pull requests.
#
# `Cargo.lock` keeps `linguist-generated` but *not* `-diff`: a lockfile change is exactly what a
# reviewer should be reading, since it is how a dependency actually enters the build.
openapi.json                 linguist-generated=true -diff
crates/api-client/src/lib.rs linguist-generated=true -diff
.sqlx/*.json                 linguist-generated=true -diff
Cargo.lock                   linguist-generated=true

# The repository is developed on Windows and built on Linux. Normalise line endings in the
# index so a checkout on either platform produces the same content hash, and keep the shell
# scripts and Dockerfiles LF whatever the developer's `core.autocrlf` says — a CRLF shebang
# line is a container that fails to start with `no such file or directory`, naming a file that
# is plainly there.
* text=auto
*.sh          text eol=lf
Dockerfile*   text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf

# `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES` is generated (`xtask notices`) and belongs with the artefacts at the top
# of this file, but its attributes are declared *here*, after `* text=auto`: the last matching
# line wins, and `eol=lf` stated above that one would be overridden by it. LF because this file
# is copied verbatim into every image and compared by CI, so a CRLF working copy would make the
# built layer differ by platform. Its diff is suppressed for the reason `openapi.json`'s is —
# 300-odd KB of licence text that only ever changes because `Cargo.lock` did, and *that* diff is
# the one worth reading.
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES  linguist-generated=true -diff text eol=lf

# …but a fuzzing seed is an **input**, not a text file, and `text=auto` was quietly rewriting
# some of them. `git ls-files --eol fuzz/seeds/` reported two entries as `i/lf w/crlf`
# (`adapters_json_body/wrapped-in-pre` and its entity-escaped sibling), so the bytes libFuzzer
# read on Windows were not the bytes it read on Linux. For a corpus that is the one property
# that has to hold: a crash reproducer is a byte string, and a platform that silently inserts a
# `\r` into it produces a file that does not reproduce — while the *seed* directory quietly
# feeds two different inputs to two different developers running what they believe is the same
# command. `-text` disables both directions of conversion, so what is committed is what is read.
#
# `corpus/` is git-ignored apart from its `.gitkeep` files, and is listed anyway: the ignore
# rule is what keeps machine output out of the tree, not a statement about how bytes should be
# handled, and the two are free to change independently.
fuzz/seeds/**   -text
fuzz/corpus/**  -text
